# __GURTHANG_PROJECT_NAME__

Generated by Gurthang. The application keeps its models, services, controllers,
views, and reusable web plumbing in explicit modules you own.

## Setup

Create a PostgreSQL database, then run:

```bash
cp .env.example .env
./bin/install-tailwindcli
npm install
npm run css:build
sqlx migrate run
```

The Tailwind installer supports Linux and macOS on x86-64 and ARM64. It pins
the standalone CLI version and verifies the platform-specific SHA-256 checksum
before installing `bin/tailwindcli`.

Start the complete development environment with one command:

```bash
gurthang run
```

Open <http://127.0.0.1:3000>. `css/base.css` is the single Tailwind source for
both Tera and Inertia. The standalone `bin/tailwindcli` watcher compiles it to
`assets/css/style.css` for Tera, while Vite imports the same file for React Fast
Refresh. Tera edits reload in the running server and refresh the browser. Rust
edits incrementally rebuild and restart only the backend, then refresh the
browser. Gurthang stays alive after a compiler error and retries on the next
Rust edit. Schema migrations remain explicit: the application does not run
them automatically at startup.

The public page is rendered with Tera and includes a Datastar counter whose
response is a server-rendered `PatchElements` SSE event. Registration and login
live at `/register` and `/login`; `/dashboard` requires an authenticated,
database-backed session. Inertia automatically echoes the readable
`XSRF-TOKEN` cookie as `X-XSRF-TOKEN`. The session cookie remains `HttpOnly`,
uses `SameSite=Lax`, and becomes `Secure` when `SESSION_SECURE=true`.

Configuration is read from `.env` or the process environment. Do not commit
the real `.env` file or log `DATABASE_URL`.

For a production build:

```bash
npm run release
APP_ENV=production VITE_DEV_SERVER_URL= target/release/__GURTHANG_PROJECT_NAME__
```

The release build embeds the Vite manifest, browser assets, compiled Tera CSS,
templates, and the Inertia SSR module in the Rust executable. The result is one
application file to deploy. PostgreSQL and, when an SSR page is used, Node.js or
Bun remain environment services. Run `npm run release` again whenever frontend
assets or templates change so the new files are embedded. Database migrations
remain an explicit deployment step.

## Background jobs

The application owns a durable PostgreSQL job queue under `src/jobs`. Running
the application normally starts the web server and the worker in one process:

```bash
cargo run
```

The same artifact can run only one role when a deployment needs to scale or
restart them independently:

```bash
target/release/__GURTHANG_PROJECT_NAME__ web
target/release/__GURTHANG_PROJECT_NAME__ worker
```

Add typed job variants and their explicit handlers in `src/jobs/mod.rs`. Enqueue
immediate or scheduled jobs through `state.jobs`:

```rust
use crate::jobs::Job;

let job_id = state.jobs.enqueue(Job::PurgeExpiredSessions).await?;
```

For work caused by a database change, enqueue on the same transaction so neither
can commit without the other:

```rust
use chrono::Utc;
use crate::jobs::{Job, JobQueue};

let mut transaction = state.database.begin().await?;
// Write application data with `transaction` here.
JobQueue::enqueue_in(&mut transaction, Job::PurgeExpiredSessions, Utc::now()).await?;
transaction.commit().await?;
```

Workers claim jobs with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, execute them outside the claim
transaction, and use expiring leases, bounded exponential retries, and failed-job
retention. UUIDv7 identifiers preserve insertion locality without another crate.
Delivery is **at least once**: every handler must be safe to execute
again after a timeout or process crash. PostgreSQL notifications reduce idle
latency, while polling and persisted rows provide correctness.

`JOB_WORKERS`, `JOB_POLL_INTERVAL_MS`, `JOB_LEASE_SECONDS`, and
`JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` tune the worker. The timeout must remain shorter than the
lease. Completed and permanently failed rows are intentionally retained for
inspection; add an application-specific retention job once operational needs
are known.

## Inertia server-side rendering

Rendering is selected by each Rust page contract. Pages default to client
rendering; opt a page into SSR explicitly:

```rust
impl InertiaPage for DashboardProps {
    const COMPONENT: &'static str = "Dashboard";
    const RENDER_MODE: InertiaRenderMode = InertiaRenderMode::Ssr;
}
```

Use `InertiaRenderMode::Client` for an explicitly client-rendered page. Only an
initial visit to an SSR page invokes the SSR worker; Inertia JSON visits and
client-rendered pages do not. Node.js 22 or newer must be available when the
application contains SSR pages. Node is the default runtime. Bun is an optional
alternative selected with `INERTIA_SSR_RUNTIME=bun`; it is not an additional
service and both runtimes are never needed.

The application validates the runtime when the first SSR page is requested,
starts the embedded SSR module as a child process, and communicates with it over
private standard I/O. The worker is restarted after a failure; failed or
timed-out renders fall back to the normal client-rendered Inertia shell.

`INERTIA_SSR_TIMEOUT_MS` controls the render and startup timeout and defaults to
5000. In development, run `npm run build:ssr` before visiting an SSR page; the
backend loads `dist-ssr/ssr.mjs` so it can be rebuilt without recompiling Rust.
Restart the backend after rebuilding it.

## Page contracts

Rust structs in `src/views/inertia` own the page prop contracts. Regenerate the
TypeScript bindings and check the client with:

```bash
cargo test export_bindings
npm run typecheck
```

Run the full suite with `cargo test`. PostgreSQL integration tests run when
`TEST_DATABASE_URL` points to a disposable database; they run migrations there
and verify the user/session schema plus the complete register-dashboard-logout
flow. Without it, the database-only tests report that they were skipped while
all unit and HTTP tests still run.

The local Inertia v3 adapter implements initial HTML and JSON visits, shared
props, partial include/exclude filtering, redirects, asset-version refreshes,
and optional React SSR. Deferred, optional, merged, once, infinite-scroll and
Precognition props and history encryption are not implemented.

Use `docs/iteration-log.md` to record cold, warm, and representative edit times.
Rate limiting is not included in this proof of concept and should be added before
exposing authentication endpoints to untrusted traffic.
